# 10. Feature-Based Architecture

Business/domain functionality should live inside `features`.

Example:

```text
features/
└── products/
    ├── components/
    │   ├── ProductCard.tsx
    │   ├── ProductForm.tsx
    │   └── ProductTable.tsx
    │
    ├── hooks/
    │   ├── useProducts.ts
    │   └── useCreateProduct.ts
    │
    ├── services/
    │   └── product.service.ts
    │
    ├── schemas/
    │   └── product.schema.ts
    │
    ├── types/
    │   └── product.types.ts
    │
    └── index.ts
```

Keep feature-specific code close to the feature.

Do not put every feature's code into global folders.

---

# 11. Shared vs Feature-Specific Code

Use this decision:

```text
Used by one feature
        ↓
Keep inside feature

Used by multiple unrelated features
        ↓
Move to shared location

Generic UI
        ↓
components/ui

Generic behavior
        ↓
hooks

Generic utility
        ↓
utils

Infrastructure
        ↓
lib
```

Do not move code into global folders prematurely.

---

# 12. Component Architecture

Components should have clear responsibilities.

## 12.1 UI Components

UI components should focus on presentation.

Examples:

```text
Button
Input
Dialog
Card
Badge
Table
Dropdown
```

Use shadcn/ui components when appropriate.

Do not recreate existing shadcn/ui components unnecessarily.

---

## 12.2 Feature Components

Feature components represent business-specific UI.

Examples:

```text
UserForm
OrderSummary
ProductCard
InvoiceTable
```

These may understand feature-specific types and behavior.

---

## 12.3 Page Components

Pages compose features and layouts.

Pages should not become giant business-logic containers.

Preferred:

```text
Page
 ↓
Feature components
 ↓
Hooks
 ↓
Services/API
```

---

## 12.4 Layout Components

Layouts manage structural UI:

- Header
- Sidebar
- Navigation
- Main content
- Footer
- Responsive shell

Layouts should not contain unrelated business logic.

---

# 13. Component Size

Do not enforce an arbitrary line count.

Instead, look for responsibility complexity.

Extract a component when:

- it has a distinct responsibility
- it is reused
- it has its own meaningful state/behavior
- it makes the parent substantially easier to understand
- it represents a meaningful UI section

Do not extract tiny JSX fragments just because they occupy several lines.

---

# 14. Props

Props should be explicit and minimal.

Avoid passing giant objects when only a few values are needed.

Prefer:

```tsx
<UserCard
  name={user.name}
  email={user.email}
/>
```

when the component only needs those fields.

Avoid unnecessary prop drilling.

If many levels require the same data, evaluate:

- composition
- context
- Zustand
- query cache

Do not automatically make everything global.

---

# 15. State Management

React applications have different types of state.

Use the correct state system.

## 15.1 Local UI State

Use React state for:

- modal open/close
- tabs
- temporary form state
- dropdown state
- hover/interaction state
- component-specific UI state

Example:

```text
useState
useReducer
```

---

## 15.2 Global Client State

Use Zustand only when state genuinely needs to be shared across distant components/features.

Good examples:

- authenticated user session metadata
- global UI preferences
- application-wide client state
- persistent client workflows
- cross-feature state

Do not put every piece of state into Zustand.

---

## 15.3 Server State

API/server data should normally be handled by TanStack Query.

Examples:

- users
- products
- orders
- dashboard data
- notifications
- API resources

Do not copy server data into Zustand simply because it is accessible there.

Preferred:

```text
API
 ↓
TanStack Query
 ↓
Components
```

---

# 16. State Decision Rule

Use this decision tree:

```text
Does only one component need it?
        ↓
Local React state

Do multiple distant components need client state?
        ↓
Zustand

Does the data come from an API/server?
        ↓
TanStack Query

Is it derived from existing state?
        ↓
Compute it instead of storing duplicate state
```

Avoid duplicate sources of truth.

---

# 17. Derived State

Do not store values that can be calculated.

Bad:

```text
items
totalItems
```

when `totalItems` can be calculated from `items`.

Prefer derived values.

This prevents synchronization bugs.

---

# 18. API Architecture

Use a centralized Axios client.

Preferred flow:

```text
Component
    ↓
Feature Hook
    ↓
Feature Service
    ↓
Axios API Client
    ↓
Backend API
```

Example:

```text
features/users/hooks/useUsers.ts
        ↓
features/users/services/user.service.ts
        ↓
lib/api/client.ts
```

Components should not contain scattered raw Axios requests.

Avoid:

```tsx
axios.get(...)
```

throughout components.

---

# 19. Axios Client

Create one centralized API client.

It should own infrastructure-level concerns such as:

- base URL
- default headers
- request configuration
- response handling
- authentication integration
- common error normalization

Do not duplicate Axios configuration in every service.

---

# 20. Service Layer

Services should handle API communication.

Example responsibility:

```text
user.service.ts

getUsers()
getUser()
createUser()
updateUser()
deleteUser()
```

Services should not contain React component logic.

Avoid:

```text
Service
  ↓
setState()
  ↓
render UI
```

Services should remain framework-light where practical.

---

# 21. TanStack Query

Use TanStack Query for server state.

Use it for:

- fetching
- caching
- mutations
- invalidation
- refetching
- background synchronization
- query lifecycle

Keep query logic organized around features.

Example:

```text
features/
└── users/
    └── hooks/
        ├── useUsers.ts
        ├── useUser.ts
        ├── useCreateUser.ts
        └── useUpdateUser.ts
```

Do not create a generic `useFetchEverything()` hook that becomes responsible for unrelated APIs.

---

# 22. Query Keys

Query keys must be predictable and centralized where complexity warrants it.

Example:

```ts
const userKeys = {
  all: ["users"] as const,
  lists: () => [...userKeys.all, "list"] as const,
  list: (filters: UserFilters) =>
    [...userKeys.lists(), filters] as const,
  details: () => [...userKeys.all, "detail"] as const,
  detail: (id: string) =>
    [...userKeys.details(), id] as const,
};
```

Avoid manually duplicating string keys across files.

---

# 23. Mutations

After mutations:

1. Update/invalidate relevant queries.
2. Avoid unnecessary full-page reloads.
3. Keep server state consistent.
4. Show appropriate loading/error feedback.

Do not invalidate every query after every mutation.

Invalidate only affected data.

---

# 24. API Types

API contracts should have explicit TypeScript types.

Avoid:

```ts
any
```

unless there is a documented unavoidable reason.

Prefer:

```ts
type User = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
};
```

Keep API/domain types close to their feature when they are feature-specific.

Shared types belong in shared locations only when genuinely shared.

---

# 25. API Error Handling

Handle errors consistently.

At minimum distinguish:

```text
400 → validation/request problem
401 → authentication problem
403 → authorization problem
404 → resource not found
409 → conflict
422 → validation/business validation
429 → rate limit
5xx → server problem
network error → connectivity problem
```

Do not duplicate error interpretation across dozens of components.

Normalize common API errors centrally.

---

# 26. Loading States

API-driven UI must handle loading states.

Use appropriate UI:

- skeleton
- spinner
- disabled button
- loading indicator
- placeholder

Do not leave users with an apparently frozen interface.

---

# 27. Empty States

Differentiate:

```text
Loading
Error
Empty
Success
```

An empty result is not an error.

Example:

```text
Loading → skeleton
Error → error state
Empty → empty-state UI
Data → content
```

---

# 28. Routing Architecture

Use React Router.

Separate route composition from page implementation.

Recommended:

```text
routes/
├── index.tsx
├── public-routes.tsx
├── protected-routes.tsx
└── route-config.ts
```

Use nested routes and layouts where appropriate.

Example:

```text
/dashboard
    /dashboard
    /dashboard/settings
    /dashboard/profile
```

---

# 29. Route Protection

Authentication and authorization should be separated conceptually.

```text
Authentication
→ Is the user logged in?

Authorization
→ Is the user allowed to access this resource?
```

Do not rely solely on hiding frontend routes for security.

The backend remains the final authority for authorization.

Frontend route guards are for:

- UX
- navigation control
- preventing unauthorized UI access

They are not a security boundary.

---

# 30. Lazy Routes

Large applications should lazy-load route-level pages when beneficial.

Example:

```tsx
const Dashboard = lazy(() => import("./pages/Dashboard"));
```

Do not lazy-load every tiny component without a measurable benefit.

---

# 31. Authentication Architecture

Authentication logic should be centralized.

Avoid implementing login/session logic independently in multiple components.

Preferred:

```text
Auth API
   ↓
Auth Service
   ↓
Auth State / Session
   ↓
Protected Routes
   ↓
Application
```

Authentication-related logic should not be duplicated.

---

# 32. Forms

Forms should have a consistent architecture.

Recommended:

```text
Form Component
    ↓
Schema / Validation
    ↓
Mutation Hook
    ↓
Service
    ↓
API
```

Keep form UI separate from API implementation.

Do not place large API workflows directly inside JSX event handlers.

---

# 33. Validation

Validation should be:

- explicit
- predictable
- reusable where appropriate
- consistent with backend contracts

Do not duplicate the same complex validation rule across multiple components.

If a validation schema already exists, reuse it.

Never assume frontend validation replaces backend validation.

---

# 34. Tailwind CSS

Use Tailwind CSS consistently.

Prefer:

```tsx
<div className="flex items-center gap-4">
```

Use shared design tokens/components when styles repeat.

Avoid giant repeated class strings when a reusable component or appropriate abstraction genuinely improves maintainability.

Do not create a new CSS abstraction for every repeated class combination.

---

# 35. shadcn/ui

Use shadcn/ui as the default shared UI foundation.

Before creating a new component:

1. Check existing shadcn/ui components.
2. Check existing project components.
3. Extend an existing component if appropriate.
4. Create a new component only when needed.

Do not install another UI library for a component already available through the existing design system.

---

# 36. Lucide Icons

Use Lucide consistently.

Do not mix multiple icon libraries unless there is a documented requirement.

Do not manually recreate common icons with SVG when an appropriate Lucide icon exists.

---

# 37. Design System

The frontend should use consistent:

- typography
- spacing
- border radius
- shadows
- colors
- states
- interaction patterns
- responsive behavior

Prefer design tokens over arbitrary values when the project has a design system.

Do not invent new visual patterns when an existing component already solves the problem.

---

# 38. Accessibility

Accessibility is part of implementation, not an optional final step.

Follow:

- semantic HTML
- proper labels
- keyboard navigation
- visible focus states
- accessible buttons
- accessible dialogs
- appropriate ARIA attributes
- meaningful alt text
- sufficient color contrast

Do not use ARIA when semantic HTML already provides the correct behavior.

---

# 39. Responsive Design

Build responsive interfaces intentionally.

Support the application's required viewport sizes.

Do not assume desktop-only layouts unless explicitly required.

Prefer existing Tailwind breakpoints and project conventions.

Do not invent arbitrary breakpoints for isolated components without a real reason.

---

# 40. Performance

Performance is important, but optimization must be purposeful.

Use when appropriate:

- route-level lazy loading
- code splitting
- image optimization
- memoization
- virtualization
- debouncing
- throttling
- TanStack Query caching
- request deduplication

Do not blindly add:

```text
React.memo
useMemo
useCallback
```

everywhere.

Optimize when:

- rendering is expensive
- props cause meaningful unnecessary renders
- computations are expensive
- profiling shows a problem
- large lists require virtualization

Do not sacrifice readability for hypothetical performance.

---

# 41. Large Lists

For large datasets, consider virtualization.

Possible libraries may include:

```text
react-window
TanStack Virtual
```

But do not install a virtualization library for small lists.

Use the existing project dependency if available.

---

# 42. Debouncing and Throttling

Use debouncing for actions such as:

- search input
- autocomplete
- API-driven filtering

Use throttling for high-frequency events such as:

- scroll
- resize
- pointer/mouse events

Do not debounce every input or event without a reason.

---

# 43. Image Handling

Images should be handled intentionally.

Rules:

- use appropriate dimensions
- avoid unnecessarily huge assets
- use modern formats when appropriate
- provide alt text
- lazy-load non-critical images when appropriate
- avoid loading large images above the fold unnecessarily

Do not add image optimization libraries without need.

---

# 44. Hooks Architecture

Hooks should have one clear responsibility.

Good:

```text
useAuth()
useDebounce()
useMediaQuery()
useUsers()
useCreateUser()
```

Avoid:

```text
useApplicationEverything()
```

A hook should not simultaneously manage:

- authentication
- API requests
- modals
- notifications
- unrelated forms
- navigation
- multiple unrelated features

---

# 45. Hook Placement

Use:

```text
src/hooks/
```

for genuinely shared hooks.

Use:

```text
features/<feature>/hooks/
```

for feature-specific hooks.

Do not put feature-specific hooks into the global hooks folder just because they are hooks.

---

# 46. Utility Architecture

Utilities should be:

- small
- focused
- preferably pure
- reusable
- framework-independent when possible

Examples:

```text
formatCurrency()
formatDate()
cn()
debounce()
parseApiError()
```

Do not create:

```text
utils.ts
```

containing hundreds of unrelated functions.

Prefer focused files:

```text
utils/
├── format.ts
├── date.ts
├── validation.ts
└── object.ts
```

---

# 47. Constants

Avoid magic strings/numbers when they represent meaningful shared concepts.

Examples:

```text
ROUTES
QUERY_KEYS
STATUS_VALUES
DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE
```

But do not turn every literal into a constant.

A constant should improve:

- readability
- reuse
- maintainability
- consistency

---

# 48. Environment Variables

Never expose secrets in frontend code.

Frontend environment variables are public at runtime/build time.

Never put:

- private API secrets
- database credentials
- private keys
- service credentials
- server-only secrets

into frontend environment variables.

Use environment variables only for values that are safe to expose to the client.

Centralize environment configuration where practical.

---

# 49. Security

Never trust frontend validation for security.

The backend must enforce:

- authentication
- authorization
- permissions
- ownership
- sensitive operations

Frontend code must still avoid obvious security issues.

Be especially careful with:

```text
dangerouslySetInnerHTML
```

and user-controlled HTML.

Sanitize untrusted HTML using the project's approved sanitization mechanism.

Do not introduce HTML rendering simply to make UI easier.

---

# 50. Sensitive Data

Do not unnecessarily store sensitive data in:

```text
localStorage
sessionStorage
URL parameters
client-side global state
```

Use the project's established authentication architecture.

Never log:

- passwords
- authentication tokens
- private credentials
- sensitive personal information
- secret API keys

---

# 51. Error Boundaries

Use React Error Boundaries at appropriate application boundaries.

They should prevent a component failure from unnecessarily crashing the entire application.

Use boundaries where meaningful:

```text
Application
    ↓
Layout
    ↓
Feature/Page
```

Do not create dozens of boundaries without a reason.

---

# 52. Logging

Logging should be intentional.

Avoid:

```ts
console.log(user);
console.log(token);
console.log(response);
```

in production code.

Remove temporary debugging logs before completing a task.

If the project uses a logging/monitoring service, follow the established integration.

Do not add Sentry, LogRocket, New Relic, or another monitoring dependency without project approval.

---

# 53. TypeScript Rules

TypeScript should provide real type safety.

Prefer:

```text
explicit types at boundaries
narrow types
union types
type guards
generics where useful
```

Avoid unnecessary:

```text
any
unknown without narrowing
type assertions
non-null assertions
```

Do not use `as SomeType` simply to silence a TypeScript error.

Fix the underlying type problem where possible.

---

# 54. Avoid `any`

`any` should not be used as a shortcut.

Bad:

```ts
const data: any = response.data;
```

Prefer defining the API response type.

If `any` is genuinely unavoidable, keep its usage isolated and document why.

---

# 55. Type Organization

Keep types close to the domain they belong to.

Example:

```text
features/orders/types/order.types.ts
```

Shared application types:

```text
src/types/
```

Do not create a giant:

```text
types.ts
```

containing every type in the application.

---

# 56. Naming Conventions

Use consistent naming.

## Components

```text
PascalCase
```

Example:

```text
UserProfile.tsx
OrderTable.tsx
LoginForm.tsx
```

## Hooks

```text
useCamelCase
```

Example:

```text
useAuth.ts
useUsers.ts
useDebounce.ts
```

## Functions

```text
camelCase
```

Example:

```text
getUsers()
createOrder()
formatCurrency()
```

## Variables

```text
camelCase
```

## Constants

Use:

```text
UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
```

when they represent true constants.

## Types

Use:

```text
PascalCase
```

Example:

```text
User
UserResponse
CreateUserPayload
```

---

# 57. File Naming

Files should use consistent naming.

Recommended:

```text
UserProfile.tsx
useUsers.ts
user.service.ts
user.types.ts
user.schema.ts
user.store.ts
```

Do not randomly mix conventions.

If an existing project has an established convention, preserve it.

---

# 58. Imports

Keep imports clean and predictable.

Prefer path aliases where configured:

```text
@/features/users
@/components/ui
@/lib/api
```

Avoid excessive relative traversal:

```text
../../../../../../components
```

Do not introduce a new alias system if the project already has one.

Remove unused imports.

---

# 59. Circular Dependencies

Avoid circular imports.

Especially avoid:

```text
feature A
  ↓
feature B
  ↓
feature A
```

Shared abstractions should live at an appropriate lower-level boundary.

Do not solve circular dependencies by blindly moving files around. Understand the dependency graph first.

---

# 60. Dependency Direction

Prefer predictable dependency direction:

```text
Pages
 ↓
Feature Components
 ↓
Feature Hooks
 ↓
Feature Services
 ↓
Shared API Infrastructure
```

Shared infrastructure should not depend on feature-specific UI.

Avoid:

```text
utils → feature
shared component → business feature
API client → page
```

when the dependency can be avoided.

---

# 61. Feature Public API

When feature boundaries become complex, expose intended public APIs through:

```text
features/<feature>/index.ts
```

Avoid importing deeply into another feature's internal files unless necessary.

Prefer:

```ts
import { UserCard } from "@/features/users";
```

over:

```ts
import { UserCard } from "@/features/users/components/internal/UserCard";
```

Do not create barrel files everywhere merely for the sake of abstraction.

Use them where they improve boundaries and discoverability.

---

# 62. Business Logic

Business logic should not be scattered throughout JSX.

Bad:

```tsx
<Button
  disabled={
    user.status === "active" &&
    order.total > 100 &&
    permissions.includes("admin") &&
    ...
  }
/>
```

When logic becomes meaningful or reusable, move it to:

- a hook
- a utility
- a feature function
- a selector
- a domain-specific helper

Keep simple presentation conditions inside the component.

---

# 63. No Business Logic in Generic UI Components

Generic components should remain generic.

For example:

```text
Button
Dialog
Input
Card
Table
```

should not know about:

```text
orders
users
permissions
authentication
payments
```

Feature-specific behavior belongs to feature components.

---

# 64. No API Logic in Generic UI Components

A generic:

```text
Button
Modal
Select
DataTable
```

should not directly call APIs.

Keep infrastructure and feature behavior outside reusable UI primitives.

---

# 65. Data Flow

Default data flow:

```text
User Interaction
       ↓
Component
       ↓
Hook
       ↓
Service
       ↓
Axios Client
       ↓
API
       ↓
TanStack Query Cache
       ↓
Component
```

For global client state:

```text
Component
   ↓
Zustand Store
   ↓
Component
```

Keep data flow predictable.

---

# 66. Single Source of Truth

Do not maintain the same data in multiple state systems without a clear reason.

Bad:

```text
API data
 ↓
TanStack Query

same API data
 ↓
Zustand

same API data
 ↓
local useState
```

This creates synchronization problems.

Prefer one authoritative source.

---

# 67. Avoid Prop Drilling

Prop drilling is not automatically bad.

For one or two levels, props are often the simplest solution.

Do not introduce Context/Zustand merely because a value passes through a parent.

Use shared state only when the data genuinely needs broader ownership.

---

# 68. React Context

Context is appropriate for relatively stable cross-tree concerns such as:

- theme
- application configuration
- specific provider-level dependencies

Do not use Context as a replacement for all application state.

If the project uses Zustand for global client state, do not create competing Context stores without a reason.

---

# 69. Rendering

Keep render logic readable.

Avoid giant inline expressions.

Bad:

```tsx
{condition1 && condition2 && condition3 && ...}
```

If the logic becomes difficult to understand, extract a meaningful variable or helper.

Do not abstract simple JSX unnecessarily.

---

# 70. Effects

Use `useEffect` only for synchronization with external systems.

Examples:

- subscriptions
- browser APIs
- event listeners
- timers
- external libraries

Do not use effects merely to derive state that could be calculated during render.

Avoid effect chains such as:

```text
state A
 ↓ useEffect
state B
 ↓ useEffect
state C
 ↓ useEffect
API call
```

Prefer direct event/query-driven logic where possible.

---

# 71. Forms and Effects

Do not use `useEffect` to synchronize every form field.

Prefer the form/state system's normal mechanisms.

Keep form state predictable.

---

# 72. Async Operations

Async operations should have clear lifecycle handling:

```text
Idle
 ↓
Loading
 ↓
Success
 ↓
Error
```

Do not allow duplicate submissions.

Disable or otherwise protect actions while required mutations are in progress.

---

# 73. UI Feedback

User actions that trigger meaningful asynchronous work should provide feedback.

Examples:

```text
Save → loading state
Delete → confirmation + loading state
Upload → progress/loading state
Login → submitting state
```

Do not show unnecessary loading indicators for trivial synchronous actions.

---

# 74. Modals and Dialogs

Use a consistent dialog implementation.

Dialogs should handle:

- open/close
- focus
- keyboard interaction
- loading state
- errors
- cancellation

Do not create multiple incompatible modal systems.

---

# 75. Tables

Use a shared table/data-table foundation when multiple features require similar behavior.

Do not implement a complete new table system inside every feature.

However, do not add advanced features to every table by default.

Only implement:

- pagination
- filtering
- sorting
- selection
- bulk actions

when required.

---

# 76. Search

Search behavior should be intentional.

For API search:

```text
Input
 ↓
Debounce
 ↓
Query
 ↓
TanStack Query
```

Do not send an API request for every keystroke unless explicitly required.

---

# 77. Pagination

Do not automatically add pagination.

When required, keep pagination state and query parameters predictable.

Do not duplicate pagination logic across multiple features if a reusable abstraction genuinely makes sense.

---

# 78. File Uploads

When implementing uploads:

- validate file type when required
- validate size when required
- show upload state
- handle failure
- avoid exposing sensitive server details
- reuse existing upload infrastructure

Do not invent upload rules without a requirement.

---

# 79. Accessibility in Forms

Every meaningful form field should have an accessible label.

Errors should be associated with the relevant input where practical.

Do not rely only on color to communicate validation errors.

---

# 80. Testing Philosophy

Testing should focus on behavior rather than implementation details.

Test:

```text
What the user can do
What the user sees
How the system behaves
```

Avoid testing internal implementation details unless necessary.

---

# 81. Unit Tests

Use Vitest for focused unit tests.

Good candidates:

- utilities
- pure functions
- transformations
- business rules
- validation logic

Do not write tests for trivial framework behavior.

---

# 82. React Testing Library

Use React Testing Library for component behavior.

Prefer queries based on:

```text
role
label
text
accessible name
```

Avoid relying heavily on implementation-specific selectors.

---

# 83. Integration Tests

Use integration tests for important flows involving multiple pieces.

Examples:

```text
Form
 ↓
Mutation
 ↓
Success/Error UI
```

or:

```text
Route
 ↓
Authentication
 ↓
Page
```

---

# 84. E2E Testing

For large applications, use an E2E framework such as:

```text
Playwright
```

or an already-approved project equivalent.

Do not introduce another testing framework when one already exists without a reason.

---

# 85. Test File Placement

Tests may be colocated with their implementation when practical:

```text
UserCard.tsx
UserCard.test.tsx
```

or organized under:

```text
__tests__/
```

Follow the existing project convention.

Do not maintain two competing test structures.

---

# 86. Test Quality

A test should fail when actual behavior breaks.

Avoid tests that only prove:

```text
component renders
```

when meaningful user behavior can be tested instead.

Test important:

- success paths
- failure paths
- validation
- permissions
- loading
- empty states
- critical interactions

---

# 87. Build and Quality Checks

Before considering a significant change complete, code should be checked for:

- TypeScript errors
- lint errors
- formatting issues
- test failures
- build failures
- unused imports
- unused variables
- obvious runtime errors

Use the project's configured scripts.

Do not invent commands when `package.json` already defines them.

---

# 88. CI/CD

Production projects should have automated quality checks where appropriate.

Typical pipeline:

```text
Push / Pull Request
       ↓
Install dependencies
       ↓
Lint
       ↓
Type Check
       ↓
Tests
       ↓
Build
       ↓
Deploy
```

Do not introduce CI/CD infrastructure unless the project requires it or the user requests it.

---

# 89. Environment Configuration

Use environment-specific configuration appropriately.

Typical:

```text
.env
.env.local
.env.development
.env.production
```

Do not commit secrets.

Do not hardcode environment-specific URLs throughout the codebase.

Centralize configuration.

---

# 90. Monorepo

For genuinely large ecosystems, a monorepo may use:

```text
pnpm workspaces
Turborepo
Nx
```

Possible structure:

```text
apps/
├── web/
├── admin/
└── mobile/

packages/
├── ui/
├── config/
├── types/
└── utils/
```

Do not introduce a monorepo for a normal single application.

Use it when multiple applications/packages genuinely benefit from shared infrastructure.

---

# 91. Package Management

Use the project's existing package manager.

Do not switch:

```text
npm → pnpm
yarn → npm
pnpm → yarn
```

without explicit approval.

Do not modify lockfiles manually.

---

# 92. Dependency Upgrades

Do not upgrade packages simply because newer versions exist.

A dependency upgrade is a separate change unless required to solve the current task.

Avoid unrelated dependency updates.

---

# 93. No Random Refactoring

Never combine:

```text
Feature request
+
Architecture migration
+
Dependency upgrade
+
Formatting rewrite
+
Naming cleanup
```

unless explicitly requested.

Keep changes focused.

---

# 94. Minimal Diff Principle

Prefer the smallest change that fully solves the requirement.

Before editing ask:

```text
What is the minimum code that must change?
```

After editing ask:

```text
Did I change anything unrelated?
```

If yes, revert unrelated changes.

---

# 95. Preserve Existing Behavior

When changing an existing feature:

```text
Existing behavior
       +
Requested change
       =
Expected result
```

Do not accidentally change:

- API contracts
- navigation
- state behavior
- validation
- permissions
- UI behavior
- loading behavior

unless the task requires it.

---

# 96. File Creation Rules

Do not create a new file unless it provides a real architectural benefit.

Before creating a file ask:

```text
Can this logically belong in an existing file?
```

If yes, keep it there unless the file is becoming genuinely too complex.

Avoid:

```text
helper.ts
helper2.ts
helper3.ts
```

for trivial functionality.

---

# 97. Refactoring Rules

Refactoring is allowed when:

- required for the feature
- required to fix a bug
- required for maintainability
- required to remove genuine duplication
- required to establish a necessary architecture boundary

Refactoring is not allowed merely because the AI prefers another style.

When refactoring:

1. Preserve behavior.
2. Keep scope controlled.
3. Explain meaningful architectural changes.
4. Avoid unrelated cleanup.

---

# 98. Naming Before Abstraction

Do not create abstractions with vague names.

Avoid:

```text
Manager
Helper
Handler
Processor
Utils
Common
DataManager
ServiceManager
```

unless the responsibility is genuinely clear.

Prefer specific names:

```text
UserService
OrderCalculator
DateFormatter
PermissionGuard
```

---

# 99. Comments

Do not comment obvious code.

Bad:

```ts
// Set the user's name
setName(name);
```

Useful comments explain:

- why
- non-obvious constraints
- workarounds
- architectural decisions
- unusual browser behavior
- important business constraints

Comments should not compensate for unclear code.

---

# 100. TODOs

Do not add speculative TODOs.

A TODO should represent a known real task.

Bad:

```text
// TODO: Improve this someday
```

Good:

```text
// TODO: Replace temporary endpoint after API v2 migration
```

---

# 101. Dead Code

Do not leave:

- unused components
- unused hooks
- unused imports
- unused functions
- commented-out implementations
- obsolete API methods

Remove dead code when it is directly related to the current change.

Do not perform unrelated cleanup across the application.

---

# 102. Duplicate Sources of Configuration

Do not define the same configuration in multiple places.

Examples:

```text
API URL
route paths
query keys
permissions
status values
feature configuration
```

When duplication creates a real consistency risk, centralize it.

---

# 103. Permission Architecture

Permissions should have one clear source of truth.

Frontend permission checks are for UI behavior:

```text
show/hide
enable/disable
route access
```

Backend authorization remains authoritative.

Do not duplicate permission logic in dozens of components.

Prefer a centralized permission utility/hook when the application needs it.

---

# 104. Role-Based UI

When roles exist, avoid hardcoding role checks everywhere.

Bad:

```tsx
if (user.role === "admin") ...
```

repeated across many files.

Prefer a centralized authorization abstraction when the application has meaningful role complexity.

However, do not build a complete permission framework if the application only has one or two simple checks.

---

# 105. Internationalization

If i18n is required, user-facing text should be handled through the project's approved i18n solution.

Do not introduce an i18n library unless required.

Do not build an i18n abstraction for an application that only has one language.

---

# 106. Dates, Currency and Formatting

Formatting logic should be consistent.

Do not format the same data differently across unrelated components.

Prefer shared formatting utilities when formatting becomes repeated.

Do not introduce a large library for a simple formatting requirement unless justified.

---

# 107. URL State

Use URL state when the state should survive:

- refresh
- sharing
- browser navigation
- bookmarking

Examples:

```text
search
filters
page
sort
tab
```

Do not put all UI state into URL parameters.

Use the appropriate state mechanism for each piece of state.

---

# 108. Browser Storage

Use browser storage intentionally.

Before using:

```text
localStorage
sessionStorage
IndexedDB
```

determine whether persistence is actually required.

Do not store everything in localStorage.

Do not store sensitive secrets unnecessarily.

---

# 109. Accessibility and Keyboard Behavior

Interactive elements must remain usable by keyboard.

Do not implement clickable `<div>` elements when a semantic `<button>` or `<a>` is appropriate.

Prefer semantic HTML before adding ARIA.

---

# 110. Responsive Components

Reusable components should not make assumptions about a specific page unless they are explicitly feature-specific.

Avoid hardcoded:

```text
desktop-only width
fixed viewport assumptions
page-specific spacing
```

inside generic components.

---

# 111. UI State Consistency

Shared UI patterns should behave consistently.

For example:

```text
Loading buttons
Error messages
Toast notifications
Dialogs
Form errors
Empty states
Skeletons
```

Do not create a different implementation for each feature when a shared system already exists.

---

# 112. API Request Cancellation

For long-running or rapidly changing requests, consider cancellation.

Especially useful for:

- search
- autocomplete
- rapidly changing filters

Use the existing Axios/TanStack Query mechanisms.

Do not add custom cancellation infrastructure unnecessarily.

---

# 113. Race Conditions

Be careful with asynchronous requests where newer data can be overwritten by older responses.

TanStack Query should handle server-state concerns where applicable.

For custom async behavior, ensure stale results cannot incorrectly overwrite current state.

---

# 114. Optimistic Updates

Use optimistic updates only when:

- UX benefits meaningfully
- rollback behavior is reliable
- mutation semantics are well understood

Do not use optimistic updates for every mutation.

---

# 115. Caching

Use TanStack Query caching for server state.

Do not create a second custom caching system unless there is a real requirement.

Avoid excessive cache complexity.

---

# 116. API Data Transformation

Transform API data at a clear boundary.

Prefer:

```text
API Response
 ↓
Service / mapper when required
 ↓
Application model
 ↓
UI
```

Do not scatter API-specific transformations throughout JSX.

Only introduce mapping layers when the API/application models genuinely differ.

---

# 117. Feature Boundaries

Features should not deeply depend on each other's internals.

Prefer:

```text
Feature A
   ↓
Feature B public API
```

instead of:

```text
Feature A
   ↓
Feature B internal file
```

If two features constantly depend on each other, reconsider the boundary.

---

# 118. Shared Component Rules

A component should become shared only when:

- multiple features genuinely need it
- behavior is stable
- API is clear
- abstraction reduces duplication

Do not make a component global because it might be reused someday.

---

# 119. Generic Components

Generic components must not contain hidden business assumptions.

For example:

```text
DataTable
```

should not automatically know about:

```text
Users
Orders
Products
Invoices
```

Feature code provides the data and behavior.

---

# 120. Architecture Decision Rule

When choosing between two valid solutions:

Prefer, in this order:

```text
Existing project pattern
        ↓
Simple solution
        ↓
Reusable solution
        ↓
Scalable solution
        ↓
Complex abstraction
```

Do not jump directly to the most sophisticated architecture.

---

# 121. New Architecture Approval

If a task would require introducing a fundamentally new architectural pattern, stop and explain the change before implementing it.

Examples:

- new state management system
- new API architecture
- new routing architecture
- new UI framework
- new design system
- monorepo migration
- repository pattern
- event bus
- complex dependency injection
- major folder restructuring

Do not silently redesign the application.

---

# 122. Existing Library First

Before adding:

```text
library
framework
utility
component
abstraction
```

check what already exists.

Priority:

```text
Existing project code
        ↓
Existing dependency
        ↓
Native platform/React
        ↓
New dependency
```

---

# 123. Task Execution Workflow

For every non-trivial task:

### Step 1 — Understand

Read the request carefully.

Identify:

- requested behavior
- affected feature
- expected UI
- constraints
- explicit exclusions

### Step 2 — Inspect

Search existing code.

Check:

- components
- hooks
- services
- stores
- routes
- types
- utilities
- API patterns

### Step 3 — Plan

Determine:

- files to change
- files to create
- existing code to reuse
- state ownership
- API flow

Keep the plan proportional to task complexity.

### Step 4 — Implement

Make focused changes.

### Step 5 — Verify

Check:

- TypeScript
- lint
- tests where applicable
- build where appropriate
- obvious runtime issues

### Step 6 — Review

Ask:

```text
Did I add unnecessary code?
Did I duplicate existing logic?
Did I change unrelated code?
Did I introduce unnecessary dependencies?
Did I change existing naming?
Did I assume anything not specified?
```

---

# 124. Before Coding Checklist

Before writing code, verify:

```text
[ ] I understand the requested task.
[ ] I know the affected feature.
[ ] I searched for existing related code.
[ ] I checked existing components.
[ ] I checked existing hooks.
[ ] I checked existing services.
[ ] I checked existing stores.
[ ] I checked existing types.
[ ] I checked existing dependencies.
[ ] I know where the new code belongs.
[ ] I am not duplicating existing functionality.
[ ] I am not expanding the scope.
```

---

# 125. Before Finishing Checklist

Before finishing:

```text
[ ] Requested functionality is implemented.
[ ] Existing functionality is preserved.
[ ] No unrelated refactoring was performed.
[ ] No duplicate implementation was introduced.
[ ] No unnecessary dependency was added.
[ ] Naming conventions are preserved.
[ ] TypeScript types are correct.
[ ] Loading state is handled where required.
[ ] Error state is handled where required.
[ ] Empty state is handled where required.
[ ] Accessibility is considered.
[ ] Responsive behavior is considered.
[ ] No debug logs remain.
[ ] No dead code was introduced.
[ ] Relevant tests/checks were considered.
```

---

# 126. User Requirement Priority

When requirements conflict, follow this priority:

```text
1. Explicit user requirement
2. Existing project behavior/architecture
3. Existing API/backend contract
4. These architecture rules
5. General best practices
6. AI preference
```

AI preference is always the lowest priority.

Do not replace an explicit user requirement with a preferred architecture.

---

# 127. Ambiguity Rule

Not every ambiguity requires a question.

Use this decision:

```text
Can existing code answer it?
        ↓
YES → inspect and follow existing behavior

Can the requirement be safely implemented without guessing?
        ↓
YES → implement the simplest solution

Would different interpretations materially change the result?
        ↓
YES → ask the user
```

Do not ask unnecessary questions when the answer is already clear from the codebase.

---

# 128. Scope Boundary Rule

Every task has a boundary.

If the task is:

```text
Fix login button loading state.
```

The scope is:

```text
Login button loading behavior.
```

It is NOT automatically:

```text
Authentication refactor
Form redesign
API redesign
Toast redesign
Routing redesign
Global state migration
```

Stay within scope.

---

# 129. Bug Fix Rule

When fixing a bug:

1. Reproduce/understand the current behavior.
2. Identify the actual cause.
3. Fix the root cause.
4. Avoid unrelated refactoring.
5. Preserve surrounding behavior.
6. Check affected paths.

Do not mask a bug with arbitrary UI/state changes.

---

# 130. Regression Awareness

When modifying shared code, consider all usages.

Before changing:

```text
shared component
shared hook
shared utility
API client
auth state
route guard
design token
```

search for its consumers.

A shared change can have a wide impact.

---

# 131. No Blind File Replacement

Do not replace an entire file when only a small section needs changing.

Preserve:

- existing imports
- unrelated functions
- comments
- configuration
- established patterns

unless replacement is genuinely necessary.

---

# 132. No Blind Rename

Do not rename files, variables, components, routes, or APIs simply for stylistic preference.

Rename only when:

- requested
- required for correctness
- required to remove a real ambiguity
- part of an explicitly requested refactor

---

# 133. No Hidden Behavior

Reusable abstractions should behave predictably.

Avoid components/hooks that silently:

- mutate global state
- trigger unrelated API requests
- navigate users
- show notifications
- modify storage

unless that behavior is explicitly part of their responsibility.

---

# 134. Separation of Concerns

Maintain clear boundaries:

```text
UI
↓
Presentation behavior
↓
Feature logic
↓
Server communication
↓
Infrastructure
```

Do not collapse everything into one file.

But also do not create unnecessary layers for trivial features.

---

# 135. Recommended Architecture Diagram

```text
                    ┌──────────────────────┐
                    │        Routes        │
                    │ React Router         │
